>> B1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.

Below is the new/changed variables:
struct lock filesys_lock; 
struct process_info {
  char *file_name;
  struct process_descriptor *procdesc;
  struct semaphore load_sema; 
  struct semaphore add_sema; 
  bool load_success;
};
struct process_descriptor {
  tid_t tid; 
  int exit_status;
  bool exited; 
  struct semaphore wait_sema; 
  bool waited_on; 
  struct list_elem elem;
  struct list children; 
  int ref_count;
  struct lock ref_lock;
};

Below is the explaination for each new variable:
filesys_lock is a global lock to ensure exclusive file system operations since any code operation with file is critical session. 
process_info is a short-lived struct that exists mainly to coordinate process creation 
file_name is the name of the executable to load.
procdesc points to the newly created process’s descriptor.
load_sema synchronizes the load process.
add_sema ensures child is listed before exiting.
load_success indicates if the child loaded successfully.
process_descriptor is a longer-lived struct that tracks essential state for each user process (thread)
tid is the thread identifier for single-threaded process.
exit_status signals final exit code of the process.
exited marks if the process has completed.
wait_sema allows parent to wait for child termination.
waited_on is a flags that wait() was already invoked once by the parent.
elem embeds this descriptor into the parent’s children list.
children holds descriptors of child processes spawned by this process.
ref_count prevents premature deallocation (parent + self)
ref_lock synchronizes changes to ref_count, avoiding race conditions.
