>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

For reading user data, first we will read and validate the reading target 
and amount of data we want to read from the argument (it could either 
be keyboard or a specific file). If reading from stdin, we read bytes 
with input_getc() into a kernel buffer. If just reading from a regular
file, we lock the file system, call file_read() into a kernel buffer, 
then unlock. Lastly for both methods, we put kernel buffer data to user space.


For writing the user data, we also validate all the user-input arguments (buffer
length, and file descriptor etc). After that, we write byte-to-byte to the 
kernel buffer. If user chooses to output to stdout, we call putbuf to print.
Otherwise, we lock, call file_write() to write to user-indicated file 
and unlock the file. 


