---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?
Our code ensures that the parent process cannot return from the system call 
before the child executable finishes loading with the load_sema semaphore 
found in the aux process_info struct. The child process increments the semaphore 
after it loads and attempts to decrement a semaphore before returning, which 
the parent process will increment after it checks the load_success boolean. The 
success/failure status is passed back to the parent through the aux struct 
used in thread_create, process_info.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?
We ensure proper synchronization when P calls wait(C) with the wait_sema 
semaphore initialized to 0. C increments the semaphore right before it exits. 
If wait is called before C exits, the parent will block until C does exit. If 
wait is called after C exits, the semaphore will have a value of 1, and the 
parent will simply pass through. We ensure that resources are freed exactly once 
right before they go out of scope by keeping a reference count ref_count in the 
child process descriptor, initalized to 2. When a process exits, it will decrement 
ref_count, and free the resources if the count becomes 0. The process will also 
decrement the ref_count of all its children since it will never call wait. If 
P terminates without waiting before C, then C will decrement its ref_count to 0 
upon exit and free its process descriptor. If P terminates without waiting after C 
exits, then P will decrement C's ref_count to 0 in cleanup and free C's process 
descriptor. This covers all the cases of wait/exit in parent/child relationships.

