>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?
We chose to implement access to user memory through inline assembly from the kernel 
as described in the assignment document. This method allowed for safe reading and 
writing from bytes in a user space buffer with graceful handling of page faults. In 
addition to this, we also defined functions validate_string, validate_buffer, 
translate_uvaddr, and kernel_buffer_copy to cleanly verify that all data provided on 
the stack as arguments were in user memory and mapped to physical pages. The advantage 
of these methods allowed for comprehensive checking in a modular fashion. 

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?
Our file descriptor tables comes at a pre-defined size mapping the file descriptor 
index to the file pointer. The advantage of this strategy is simple and efficient array 
lookups, in addition to process isolation since each process has its own table. A clear 
disadvantage to this, however, is that the table is fixed-capacity, and requires a linear-
time insertion to avoid external fragmentation in the array. There is also no file descriptor 
inheritance when a process calls fork, which should be supported in an operating system.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?
We did not change it, since Pintos has a single-threaded design. This means that every thread, 
except for the initial thread that was created before user process bootstrapping, has a one-to-one 
association with a process. 

